 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "In 1831 a 24-year-old Isambard Kingdom Brunel won the competition to bridge the Avon Gorge. Money ran out, Brunel died in 1859, and his fellow engineers finished it in 1864 as his memorial, using chains from his old Hungerford Bridge in London. The deck hangs about 75 metres above the river.",
   "plaque": "avon_gorge_clifton_bridge",
   "title": "Clifton Suspension Bridge"
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "CLIFTON SUSPENSION BRIDGE. Pedestrians free. Please do not lean over the rail to see how far down it is. It is a long way."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "The Avon Gorge has whitebeams found nowhere else on Earth, among them the Bristol whitebeam and Wilmott's whitebeam, clinging to the limestone. Rare plants grow here too, like Bristol rock-cress and the round-headed leek, called the Bristol onion. Look, but leave them be.",
   "plaque": "avon_gorge_whitebeams",
   "title": "The Gorge's Own Trees"
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "LEIGH WOODS. Ancient woodland on the Somerset side of the gorge, with an Iron Age hillfort, Stokeleigh Camp, hidden in the trees."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 35,
   "text": "Every August since 1979, hundreds of hot-air balloons gather on the Ashton Court estate here and take off together at dawn and dusk in mass ascents. It is one of the biggest balloon events in Europe, and at the Night Glow the tethered balloons light up in time to music.",
   "plaque": "avon_gorge_balloon_fiesta",
   "title": "Bristol International Balloon Fiesta"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "ASHTON COURT ESTATE. Deer park, golf, mountain bikes and, one weekend a year, half the sky."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "Legend says two giant brothers, Goram and Vincent, dug the gorge, and Vincent's cave is in the cliff below. The Observatory began as a windmill that burned down in 1777; William West rebuilt it and put a camera obscura on top in 1829, and dug the tunnel down to the cave.",
   "plaque": "avon_gorge_giants_cave",
   "title": "Giant's Cave and the Observatory"
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "Peregrine falcons nest on the cliff ledges here, as they have done on and off for centuries. A peregrine folds its wings and dives on pigeons at well over 300 kilometres an hour, the fastest animal on the planet. Watch the ledges below the Observatory in spring.",
   "plaque": "avon_gorge_peregrines",
   "title": "Peregrines of the Gorge"
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 6,
   "text": "From 1893 to 1934 a funicular railway ran up a tunnel inside this cliff, from Hotwells to Clifton, its cars worked by water ballast. In the Second World War the tunnel sheltered the BBC, ready to keep broadcasting if London was bombed.",
   "plaque": "avon_gorge_rocks_railway",
   "title": "Clifton Rocks Railway"
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "The Avon here rises and falls with the Severn Estuary, which has one of the biggest tidal ranges in the world, over 14 metres at the biggest spring tides. At low tide the river shrinks to a trickle between steep, shining mudbanks. Do not go down on to the mud.",
   "plaque": "avon_gorge_tides",
   "title": "A Tidal River"
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "THE DOWNS. Four hundred acres of open grass where Bristol walks its dogs. The goats on the cliffs are on scrub duty: they eat the brambles that would smother the rare flowers."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 60,
   "text": "PUBLIC FOOTPATH. Bristol and the Avon Gorge, north. Bath, behind you, with its buns."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "CIDER ORCHARD. Somerset apples. The trees are wassailed every January: songs, toast in the branches and a lot of noise to wake them up and scare off bad spirits."
  }
 ],
